WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:04.080
Chris Hardwick: This is 
the Star Wars: The Force Awakens panel

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.080
Um...
[Crowd cheering]

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.040
I know a lot of you start --

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.420
Oh, there is the graphic 
that proves I'm not lying.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:14.380
I'm not a liar.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.880
I know a lot of you guys waited
a long time to be here.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.400
I mean like ... decades, really.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.480
And, so I'm also here to tell you that
you're not gonna disappointed.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.680
And I want you to know before we get into this, 
really quickly and its not about me,

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.040
but I just want you to know how special
and how emotional this is for me.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.300
I am old enough to have seen
Star Wars in the theatre, which is--

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.400
[Cheering in crowd]

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.440
Why would you cheer that.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.600
It sucks to get older,
but here we are.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.080
My Dad took me, I stood on the seat,
it completely changed my life,

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.820
and informed pretty much
everything that came after,

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.620
I know the experience is
 the same for you.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:56.040
So, there's gonna be some amazing things
that are gonna happen,

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:59.040
and someday you will look back,
and get to tell --

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.200
Your grandkids will be like,
"Shut up, Grandpa."

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.640
"I was at the Force Awakens panel, and --"

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:04.900
[Laughter]

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.000
"Back in Ought-Fifteen."
And they'll be like, "Shut up."

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.800
So, I want you to really
think about this,

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.400
and experience it, and enjoy it
as it's happening, ok?

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.620
Because this is an experience
that just this group of us will have

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.620
for the rest of our lives.
So, uh --

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.620
[Audience cheering]

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:25.620
J.J. Abrams:
Haha, you're welcome.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.020
Hardwick: Oh, did you
give them donuts?

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.860
Kathleen Kennedy:
Got the donuts and the coffee.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:32.800
Abrams: Donuts!

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.460
Lawrence Kasdan:
Thank you.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.060
Hardwick: I mean, I feel like
they were gonna see the movie anyway,

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.820
but that's very nice of you.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.820
"I was on the fence,
but he gave me donuts.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.200
So I guess I'll go
see Star Wars."

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:45.620
Um --

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.440
I wanna start --
I'm sure they were good. Alright.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.440
Kathleen, I want to start with you,

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.540
and I want you to talk
a little bit about --

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:56.800
I mean, Star Wars was one of the things
that helped usher in the Comic-Con age.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:58.860
So, can you talk about the fandom,

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.680
and the presence of Star Wars
throughout the years?

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.860
Kathleen Kennedy: Now, obviously,
I think a lot of people out there know,

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:10.960
that Star Wars is 100% dependent
on the fans in this room

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.020
and all over the world,
since 1976 --

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:16.640
[Cheering and clapping]

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.620
Kennedy: In fact -- in fact,
when the movie was ready to come out,

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:24.480
it was really the fans
that built the momentum

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.980
around the release
of New Hope.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:31.060
So we are right back,
in full circle,

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:34.680
and we want to say thank you,
to all of you.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.060
Hardwick: Yeah.
Abrams: Yeah.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.640
[Clapping and cheering]

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:41.440
Hardwick: So, just to
give us a timeline,

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.440
'cause we're still
a handful of months out

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.220
from being able to see this,

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:47.220
are you freaking out?
Where are you in production?

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:48.660
At what point?

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:51.480
Abrams: We are --
we're in -- we're editing,

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.340
we have a cut of the movie,
we're doing -- uh --

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.820
We're in this extraordinary moment
where we're doing fine tuning,

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:00.840
and I say extraordinary, because
in this business, a lot of times,

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.820
there are release dates
before there are scripts,

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:05.820
and to have the time

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:10.880
and to have Disney give us the time
to do it right is not a small thing.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:14.940
And so, we're working hard to make the movie 
more of what it wants to be.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:16.940
Hardwick: Great. 
Um, Mr. Kasdan.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:18.680
Lawrence Kasdan: Yes.
Hardwick: Welcome, by the way,

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.680
I don't think you've been 
to a Comic-Con before, have you?

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.240
[Clapping and cheering]

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:25.620
Kasdan: Thank you.
Harwick: He's --

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.000
Technically, you're our dad,
in a weird sort of way.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.500
Kasdan: Not in age, [indistinct].

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:36.160
Hardwick: No. But, can you talk
a little bit about, when you guys --

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:42.040
I mean, first of all, the thing I think
you need to understand about J.J. is that

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:44.380
this -- he really cares about this a lot.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:47.280
It's as important to him
as it is to you and to me,

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:50.580
and so, I want to talk about the experience
of you guys coming together.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.840
You bringing in legacy Star Wars,

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:56.840
and then, J.J., you --
taking on the responsibility

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:00.600
of meeting together and figuring out how to
bring new characters into the universe.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:03.420
Kasdan: Yeah, well, I --
the guy that brought me in

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:07.220
is the genius that
brings us all here.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:09.220
It was George Lucas.
He was --

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:10.820
[Cheering]

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:14.040
Kasdan: Absolute, breakthrough,
monster genius,

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.220
and none of us would be here
if it weren't for him.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:19.420
And he called me, and said,
"Will you do this for me?"

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:21.420
And we started to do
"Empire Strikes Back,"

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:23.740
and then we did
"Return of the Jedi."

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:26.020
And then thirty years passed.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:29.720
And those movies just, like, never went
out of my life or my consciousness.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.520
And then a call came again.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:32.920
Said, "Will you come back?

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:35.980
And meet these characters
again, thirty years later?"

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:41.260
And so there wasn't even any disjunction 
between my life and this timeline.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:46.140
And when we got J.J.
to say he would do that,

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:48.620
I went berserk.
Because I --

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:51.340
Abrams: In a bad way.
Kasdan: In a bad way.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:56.240
I thought he was the funniest, most talented,
most perfect choice for this movie.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:59.040
And then he and I wound up
spending a year,

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.780
walking around, writing,
figuring it out,

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:03.560
and it's been a total gas.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:06.660
Hardwick: J.J., how do you
push the fandom out of the way,

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:10.180
to focus on the work?
When you're working with Larry.

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:14.500
Abrams: I mean --
Star Wars is, like you said,

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:16.400
it's something that
is so deeply ingrained

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.240
and so important
to so many people --

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.700
You know, I asked my mom to make me
a Jawa costume for my thirteenth Halloween,

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:27.640
and uh -- I did the eyes,
but she did the costume.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:29.860
And it was great.
And, uh --

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:33.400
I've been a fan since
I was a kid, a little kid.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:36.360
And -- there is nothing normal

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:40.240
about getting to write a movie
with Lawrence Kasdan.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.960
There's nothing normal about
getting to work with Kathleen Kennedy,

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.720
or to direct a Star Wars movie.

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:48.100
I've sat down with John Williams

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:51.160
to show him scenes
from a Star Wars movie

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:53.540
he hadn't seen yet.

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:55.400
That I directed.

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:58.220
There's nothing normal

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:00.080
about any of what's been happening.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:01.420
Including this moment.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:03.420
And by the way, you guys look awesome
and thank you for waiting so long to be here.

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:05.140
It's really -- thank you.
Kasdan: Yes, thank you.

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:07.640
[Audience and panel clapping]
[Audience cheering]

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:10.040
Hardwick: I think --

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:13.160
I think, one of the things that's been
so [indistinct] to watch is that

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:16.240
you really seeded out
a lot of the stuff.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:17.880
I mean, you put stuff out
on Instagram,

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.140
and I know you made
this great Omaze video,

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:23.080
and when people started to see
glimpses of what you were building,

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:27.260
and they saw, "holy crap, he's actually built,
like, these are practical effects.

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:29.100
We're seeing, like, 
real things, and--"

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.100
which is astonishing
these days.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:32.800
Abrams: Well, it's
an important piece of this,

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:34.120
and we've talked
about it a little bit,

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:35.820
but we did this video

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:38.240
for UNICEF, through
this group, Omaze,

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:41.060
and it was a really
successful endeavor.

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:44.720
We raised $5 million,
thanks to your help,

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:46.640
for UNICEF
Innovation Labs

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:48.920
and it's a big deal.
So thank you for that.

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:51.060
Um -- But when we first
started shooting the movie,

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:52.660
we did this video
that went out

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:55.940
where I was -- we were all in Abu Dhabi,
and I was standing there,

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:58.540
and there was a creature
in the scene,

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:00.620
and some people, uh --

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.660
There it is.
Um, and,

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:06.440
some people were [indistinct],
"oh, great, you made creatures,"

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:08.680
and other people thought,
"oh, it's a CG thing," and --

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:09.780
Uh --

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:12.080
We wanted to show you
it wasn't CG,

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:13.760
and this is just one
of many characters,

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:15.760
so we actually brought
this character, we call Bobbajo,

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:17.760
as a nickname, so let's --
should we bring Bobbajo out?

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:19.260
See what happens?
Hardwick: Yeah, I think so, absolutely.

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:22.260
Abrams: Let's see.
[Audience cheering]

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:24.040
Abrams: All right.
Everyone cross their fingers.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:25.940
Cross your fingers.
Here we go.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:32.800
All right.
So, this character --

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:36.860
Here he comes.
Hardwick: Amazing.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:40.840
Let's see.
No, I don't want --

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:43.520
Abrams: This character was
built by Neal Scanlan

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:46.020
and the creature department.

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:48.620
Let's see if this works.
Hardwick: Yes, thanks. All right.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:50.180
Kennedy: Here we go.
Thank you.

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:52.180
Hardwick: So --
Kennedy: I think so.

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:53.460
Abrams: Want to talk
about Neal Scanlan?

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:55.940
Kennedy: Ok, yeah, Neal --
Neal Scanlan...

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:59.500
These guys have done
an unbelievable job, as you can see.

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:03.580
We're gonna hope that he does not fall off
the stage here, so I'm just kinda looking--

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:05.300
Hardwick: I'm keeping
an eye on him,

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:07.200
and I will give my life
to save him.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:08.660
Kennedy: Yeah, he --
[Audience laughter]

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:11.200
Bobbajo's a little slow,

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:14.020
so we're just gonna
give him a little....

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:15.680
Abrams: Huh!
Kennedy: A bit worrisome here.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:17.020
Hardwick: So --
Abrams: How's it going?

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:19.640
Good to see you. Hi, how are you.
Hello, how you doin'. Don't eat that.

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:21.640
Don't eat that.
So, uh --

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:23.460
This is one of, uh --

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:25.320
of hundreds of creatures

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:27.320
that they built
for this film.

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:32.640
And what's amazing is
seeing the people who, uh --

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:34.640
operate these things.

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:37.380
And the real benefit
of having creatures like this --

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:39.060
and you can see
that character right there,

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:42.420
you know, there are five people
who are operating that creature.

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:44.420
And a lot of these creatures are even,
are background creatures.

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:46.060
They're not even
the main event.

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:47.540
But what's so cool
about them

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:50.660
is it gives the actors
who are in scenes

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:54.640
things to actually deal with,
interact with, play with, respond to.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:55.380
Uh --

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:57.940
It also helps -- 
even things like light,

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:00.460
the way light reflects
on the set,

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:02.260
and on the characters.
So it's a big deal.

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:04.460
All these characters, these creatures
you're seeing, were built --

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:07.260
Kennedy: No, and it's amazing, too,
because even though we're not --

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:09.820
we decided not to use CG,

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:14.400
the robotic technology has
really advanced, as you can see.

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:16.500
[Audience clapping and cheering]
Hardwick: Well, yeah, I mean --

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:18.020
And especially --

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:23.400
[Audience cheering intensifies]

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:25.460
[A smaller creature on the larger creature
makes a short honking noise]

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:26.820
Kennedy: Here he comes.

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:29.060
Hardwick: Oh, he's going to walk all the way
back across. Please don't fall this time.

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:31.540
Abrams: This is where it gets ugly. But --

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:33.160
Kennedy: This is not easy.

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:37.400
Hardwick: For anyone who grew up
with the original Star Wars,

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.840
and it was part of the charm
of the movie,

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:43.200
was being able to see these characters look
as though they were -- had come to life.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:46.800
And I think CG certainly has its place,
but seeing a practical effect --

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:50.400
I mean, even seeing that, seeing him
wiggle his nose in that way,

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:56.180
it does something emotionally that reminds us
of the first time we saw Star Wars.

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:58.180
Kennedy: And the friends, 
and the friends up here [indistinct].

00:09:58.180 --> 00:09:59.840
Hardwick: And the little friends,
along the side.

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:01.180
[Abrams chuckles]

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:03.500
Abrams: And, there you go. Uh oh.
Kennedy: Ok. Oh, you resting?

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:06.220
Abrams: Oh dear God.
[Kennedy and Hardwick laugh]

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:07.680
Kennedy: Oh!
Hardwick: Yeah.

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:09.980
Abrams: I knew it.
Hardwick: That's ok, I'll wait.

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:13.980
All right. Excellent.

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:16.200
And would you -- you said
this was Neal Scanlan.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:18.520
Kennedy: Yeah.
Abrams: Yeah, Neal Scanlan and his team --

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:20.320
the most amazing creature shop.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:21.440
Hardwick: I'm fine.

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:25.100
Abrams: And there he goes.
Hardwick: All right, and there we go.

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:26.840
Huge hand for Bobbajo.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:28.840
[Panel and audience clapping, cheering]

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:39.280
I mean, essentially,

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:42.200
this is the -- when you're working
with these types of practical effects,

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:45.540
I mean, there's -- it's basically just
whatever limits your imagination has, right?

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:47.720
I mean, you can say, 'we want
to build this, I want to see this,

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:49.140
I need to populate
the world in this way.'

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:51.040
Abrams: Well, here's the thing.
There's -- of course, it's Star Wars,

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:52.920
there's going to be
a ton of visual effects.

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:54.920
There will be CG,
you can't avoid it.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:57.700
And -- but the important thing for us
was that we make this authentic,

00:10:57.700 --> 00:10:59.080
that we tell the story,

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:01.080
and allow the actors to interact with

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:03.320
and be on a set with

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:06.680
as much authentic, real,
tangible construction as possible.

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:09.300
So sets, props, creatures.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:11.860
And it just -- 
it helped the entire process

00:11:11.860 --> 00:11:14.840
look and feel like it was
actually on screen, because it was.

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:17.400
Hardwick: So, sort of the way
the panel's gonna work a little bit

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:18.960
is, we're gonna -- 
[Scattered applause]

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:20.640
Oh, nice. Hey, sure,
give it up. Come on.

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:21.940
Abrams: Authenticity!

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:23.940
[Audience cheering and clapping]

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.360
Hardwick: We're gonna take questions
sort of throughout the panel,

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:32.880
so that everyone can
sort of focus a little bit.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:37.460
So let's go and take a couple of questions
for Larry and J.J. and Kathleen.

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:39.740
Um, this first, uh -- hello.

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:45.680
What's your name?
Like I don't know.

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:46.720
&gt;&gt; [In scratchy voice]
Batman!

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:48.640
Hardwick: Yeah, I know it's Batman.
That was a dumb question.

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:50.180
[Panel and audience laughter]
Hardwick: Yeah.

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:52.640
What's your real name?

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:54.640
&gt;&gt; Batman!
Hardwick: Batman. Yeah, ok, that's what I thought.

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:59.620
So, Mr. Batman, 
what is your question?

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:01.400
&gt;&gt; [Maintaining scratchy voice]
My question is for Mr. J.J. Abrams.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:03.400
[Hardwick laughs]
[Audience laughs]

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:09.000
And it is, what influences did you take,

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:12.600
what amalgam of concepts
did you draw upon,

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:15.940
to make this film?
Whether it be the comics,

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:19.120
video games,
even other movies?

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:21.880
Hardwick: First of all, way to commit.
That was amazing.

00:12:21.880 --> 00:12:22.800
[Audience and panel laughter]

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:26.580
I'm very proud that you got through
that whole thing, as Batman.

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:30.440
J.J.?
Abrams: Um --

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:32.160
I couldn't really
understand you.

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:33.820
No, I could. I could.
I got it. I got it.

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:35.820
Hardwick: What he said was,
[imitates scratchy voice, speaking unintelligibly]

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:37.560
Abrams: It was good.
Uh -- uh --

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:40.120
First of all, awesome costume,
and great question.

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:42.840
What I think -- and Larry,
I think you could answer this as well.

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:46.040
I mean, we tried to sit down 
and ask ourselves,

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:47.760
what feels right?

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:50.660
And the only real mandate
we had was,

00:12:50.660 --> 00:12:53.220
what is --
what delights us?

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:57.780
Doesn't mean it has to be fun all the time,
and sort of, you know, forever silly,

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:00.320
but it means it has
to be compelling.

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:04.520
And obviously
we treated the films,

00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:06.520
you know, IV, V, and VI,
especially which --

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:08.520
those were things that,
you know, for me,

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:09.920
were the films
I grew up with,

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:11.540
um, we treated those
as canon.

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:13.360
And, by the way,
that Larry wrote.

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:15.360
It's insane. To ask -- 
to be writing a script,

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:17.540
a Star Wars script,
with Larry Kasden.

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:18.820
Uh -- you know, it's like,

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:20.000
"What would Han say --?"

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:21.680
[Imitates Kasden]
"I'll tell you what Han would say."

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:22.900
[Laughter]

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:26.040
[Still imitating Kasden]
"I wrote Han. I know what Han sounds like."

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:28.220
[As self] : That's a perfect
impersonation of Larry Kasden.

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:31.720
Kasden: But when we were --
obviously, he can't do me at all.

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:33.240
[Laughter]

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:35.240
But, we won't mention it.

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:37.620
He -- When we were talking
about the light, we just --

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:40.960
we're talking about, it's something
as simple as the texture of a wall.

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:43.120
The fact that a person
casts a shadow,

00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:45.720
creature casts the shadow
as he comes through the room.

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:47.980
You can feel that
those are real weapons,

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:51.220
and those are real, uh,
stormtroopers back there.

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:52.400
It was a happy moment.

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:54.840
We had been --
we were writing very fast,

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:56.400
we were writing
really hard, and --

00:13:56.400 --> 00:13:59.520
In one month, we were walking around
Santa Monica, figuring out the story.

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:04.740
And just a couple months later,
we're sitting in the bay of a Star Destroyer,

00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:09.000
with stormtroopers
drilling in front of us.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:10.800
And still writing, of course.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:14.040
And -- it was all real.

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:15.460
It was all real.

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:17.860
And we said, "Well,
we got that part right.

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:18.960
We got that part right."

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:21.980
Abrams: It -- the answer was,
Batman, that --

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:23.500
[Hardwick chuckles]
[Audience laughter]

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:27.880
We wanted to tell a story
that would make us feel,

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:30.100
and that would be something
that would feel like a continuum

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:32.980
of the incredible story
that George started.

00:14:32.980 --> 00:14:35.060
Hardwick: "I had the weirdest dream
that I was in front of 7,000 people,

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:36.940
and then Batman asked
a question about Star Wars."

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:38.780
Like, that's the strangest --
that's my life.

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:42.280
Uh - hah - what is your - 
thank you very much.

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:44.020
Uh, what is your name?

00:14:44.020 --> 00:14:46.020
&gt;&gt; Hello, my name is
Lynette Grant,

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:49.380
and this is a dream come true
in so many ways, so.

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:50.620
Great to be here.

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:52.620
Hardwick: Thank you. Of course.
What is your question?

00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:56.340
&gt;&gt; This question is
actually for J.J., and...

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:58.340
Star Wars is precious to me.

00:14:58.340 --> 00:15:01.920
I sat on the couch with my daddy
when I was a little girl,

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:06.300
and we watched Star Wars,
and Star Trek, and Battle Beyond the Stars.

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:08.560
It's a precious legacy, 
not only to me,

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:12.580
but I'm pretty sure to a couple
of people in this room. [Chuckles]

00:15:12.580 --> 00:15:17.980
When you get such a heavy burden,
and you have this precious legacy,

00:15:17.980 --> 00:15:22.000
and you have to pay homage
to that, to us and the fans,

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:25.020
and then you've got this
new generation that you're building,

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:28.720
and trying to bring this alive for them,
how do you approach that?

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:31.000
Hardwick: No pressure.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:34.900
Abrams: I think, honestly, I feel more pressure answering your question than I do --

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:38.960
Uh -- no, it's a great question.
It really is. Uh --

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:41.760
I watched Star Wars
with my parents, too.

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:43.760
It was very much -- uh --

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:46.860
it meant very much to me,
what it means to so many of you.

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:50.700
And I feel like the answer that I can give you,
the only answer I can say is,

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:54.920
because we love it,
and we care about it so much,

00:15:54.920 --> 00:15:57.140
our job is to not be
blinded by that.

00:15:57.140 --> 00:16:00.140
Meaning, you can't just be a fan

00:16:00.140 --> 00:16:02.440
and then say, well, you're gonna
make a movie, 'cause you're a fan.

00:16:02.440 --> 00:16:05.660
It's not enough. You gotta really say,
what is a -- what's the story?

00:16:05.660 --> 00:16:07.900
I mean, I will tell you
from personal experience,

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:11.320
when you're directing a scene 
on the Millennium Falcon,

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:13.700
it doesn't make the scene good.

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:16.480
Now, it's bitchin' that it's on the Millennium Falcon. 
[Audience laughter]

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:18.120
Like, you want a scene on the Mille --

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:21.500
If I could make a suggestion:
direct scenes on the Millennium Falcon.

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:23.360
'Cause it's hugely helpful.

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:26.020
But it doesn't make the scene
automatically good.

00:16:26.020 --> 00:16:29.200
So you have to ask, what --
it's literally Storytelling 101.

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:30.940
What do the characters want?
Who are they?

00:16:30.940 --> 00:16:32.940
What makes this interesting?
What's unexpected?

00:16:32.940 --> 00:16:35.820
How do you make this --
it has to be fun, it has to be scary.

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:41.780
It has to -- the power of what's come before
is so infectious and so deep,

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:44.820
that you have to harness it,
but you can't be blinded by it.

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:48.620
And it's a constant thing,
working with Larry and with Kathy,

00:16:48.620 --> 00:16:50.620
there were all these
checks and balances, saying,

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:53.020
"That's really cool, 
but what does it mean?"

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:54.600
You know, "why are we doing this?"

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:57.260
And so, I hope I've answered
your question by saying, we tried --

00:16:57.260 --> 00:17:00.760
we worked as hard as we possibly could, 
and I can't wait to show you the movie.

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:02.260
Hardwick: Awesome.
Abrams: Thank you.

00:17:02.260 --> 00:17:05.080
Hardwick: Ok, so one more question
for this section. Hello!

00:17:05.080 --> 00:17:07.200
&gt;&gt; Yeah, my question's for J.J.

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:09.660
I just wanted to know,

00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:12.920
You did such a great job with Star Trek,
the first two movies.

00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:17.640
Are you gonna do each individual
movie as a standalone,

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:21.940
or are they gonna be tie-ins,
or it is gonna be a serious arc?

00:17:21.940 --> 00:17:23.420
Abrams: Well, I'll let Kathy answer that,

00:17:23.420 --> 00:17:27.000
because Kathy is the one who oversees
every possible thing at Lucasfilm.

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:29.080
Kennedy: Well, obviously
with the Saga films,

00:17:29.080 --> 00:17:33.120
which -- J.J. is starting the first, 
which is the Force Awakens,

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:35.620
8 and 9 will be part of that saga.

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:39.240
But we're also making
anthology films, standalone movies.

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:41.240
[Audience cheering]

00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:45.100
And Gareth Edwards actually starts
shooting in about three weeks.

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:48.460
So the first one will go
into production very soon.

00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:50.100
Hardwick: Excellent.
Thank you very much.

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:55.240
So, obviously you had made
a brief statement, a handful of days ago,

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:59.220
just sort of let people know, like, you know,
we're not really ready to show footage yet.

00:17:59.220 --> 00:18:00.920
Um, but --

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:03.800
Is there anything that you could?
I mean, is --

00:18:03.800 --> 00:18:05.580
Abrams: Yes.
Now, here's the thing.

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:07.580
Now here's the thing -- 
uh, the truth is,

00:18:07.580 --> 00:18:11.600
we aren't ready to show you
scenes from the movie,

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:13.340
or a new trailer,

00:18:13.340 --> 00:18:17.300
and we really want to wait
and do the trailer later in the fall.

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:20.420
But the -- we knew that
this was too important,

00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:23.480
coming to you, who care about this
and love this so much.

00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:29.140
And we wanted to bring something that was
as unique and unexpected as we could.

00:18:29.140 --> 00:18:32.040
We put together a piece
that you're gonna see,

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:36.500
that we hoped would bring you
into the process a little bit,

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:38.940
of what it was like to make this movie

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:40.940
and get to see some of
the people who are doing it.

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:43.140
So, uh, we have that.

00:18:43.140 --> 00:18:46.360
Hardwick: Yes, please. I saw
this last night, and I cried. Alright.

00:18:46.360 --> 00:18:48.360
[Laughter]

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:51.440
Yes, I know.
Mike's already crying.

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:56.140
Alright, so, let's see this footage,
and I think you will be very happy.

00:18:56.140 --> 00:19:01.480
I mean, that is never-before-seen
footage of behind-the-scenes

00:19:01.480 --> 00:19:03.660
of The Force Awakens.
And also,

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:06.560
I do want to point out, there is a very
special guest here in the audience.

00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:08.560
Peter Mayhew is out here.
[Audience cheering]

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:11.660
Uh -- there he is.

00:19:11.660 --> 00:19:13.660
There's Peter, over there,
by the microphone.

00:19:13.660 --> 00:19:15.660
[Audience clapping
and cheering]

00:19:21.500 --> 00:19:24.460
This is my notebook,
this is the notebook that I have.

00:19:24.460 --> 00:19:26.700
This is my notebook.

00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:28.700
[Panelists chuckling]

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:30.800
My notebook. Uh --

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:34.280
Alright. So, now we're gonna
meet some of the cast.

00:19:34.280 --> 00:19:38.560
Some of whom, I know you guys
got to talk to a little bit at Celebration.

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:40.460
So let's bring them out.

00:19:40.460 --> 00:19:42.600
Playing Finn, 
John Boyega is here!

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:44.600
[Audience clapping
and cheering]

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:49.820
Also Daisy Ridley!

00:19:49.820 --> 00:19:51.820
[Audience clapping
and cheering]

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:56.840
And as Poe Dameron,
Oscar Isaac!

00:19:56.840 --> 00:19:58.840
[Audience clapping
and cheering]

00:20:03.380 --> 00:20:06.440
Welcome. Oh yeah,
that's right! Ok, ok good.

00:20:06.440 --> 00:20:07.940
You have to bring out
your own name cards now.

00:20:07.940 --> 00:20:09.360
Female in audience: Oscar!
Hardwick: Oh-my-god!

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:12.680
Ah, welcome! 
Oscar Isaac: Hi, everybody!

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:14.680
Hardwick: How're you guys doing?

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:15.900
[Boyega, Ridley, Isaac,
"good, so good" etc]

00:20:15.900 --> 00:20:19.240
Hardwick: John, um,
can you talk a little bit about

00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:20.980
you're -- you got to
walk the floor a little bit.

00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:22.000
John Boyega: Yeah, yeah.

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:24.240
Hardwick: And so, how is it,
being on both sides?

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:25.440
Like, you know, sort of,

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:27.080
pre-Star-Wars now, 
Star Wars --

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:29.580
are you feeling -- does it feel
any different, being a fan?

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:31.460
Boyega: I mean, let's just say
this is really complicated,

00:20:31.460 --> 00:20:33.460
buying a black series
Star Wars figure out here.

00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:35.020
[Hardwick et al chuckle]

00:20:35.020 --> 00:20:37.620
I can't get through the crowd, honestly.
Thank you, J.J.

00:20:37.620 --> 00:20:39.480
Ah, but, it's been awesome. 
I mean, uh,

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:42.800
we walk the floor, we got to meet
you guys, we got to meet some fans

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:45.600
by the LucasFilm booth.
And it was amazing fun.

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:47.600
Oh, you were there?
Hey, man! Hey!

00:20:47.600 --> 00:20:50.320
Hardwick: But they didn't know that was you, right?
They didn't know that was you underneath --

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:51.500
Boyega: Oh, yeah, yeah, so, yeah,

00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:54.620
this is me, walking through
the Star Wars Celebration Hall,

00:20:54.620 --> 00:20:58.260
obviously, I was in disguise.
I couldn't afford the rest of the costume,

00:20:58.260 --> 00:21:00.980
But, uh, you know, it still worked.

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:03.960
And that guy totally didn't know
that was me, by the way.

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:05.960
He was just like, "dude,
why do you have my saber?"

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:07.780
[Hardwick and Ridley chuckle]
But we had a great time.

00:21:07.780 --> 00:21:09.780
Oh, that was you?
Oh, that was you in the picture!

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:11.500
Daisy Ridley: Hey!
Boyega: Hey, man!

00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:13.900
Hardwick: That was literally you?
Boyega: How you doing? Oh, hey!

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:15.660
[Boyega clapping]
Hey!

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:18.220
Hardwick: Oh, it was so much funnier
when we thought you didn't know!

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:20.220
Boyega: Yeah, yeah,
I thought you didn't know.

00:21:20.220 --> 00:21:21.620
[Audience member speaks
but is not near a microphone]

00:21:21.620 --> 00:21:23.320
Boyega: Oh, you shook my hand.

00:21:23.320 --> 00:21:27.060
Well, yeah, guys, yeah, man.
Thank you so much, man!

00:21:27.060 --> 00:21:28.100
I love you guys, honestly.

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:30.780
That was a great experience.
I enjoyed myself.

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:32.160
Hardwick: Yeah. What are the --
actually, the odds are pretty good,

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:33.560
that that guy would be here.
What am I saying?

00:21:33.560 --> 00:21:34.800
Boyega: I know, right?
Yeah, yeah!

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:38.320
Hardwick: "What are the odds a Star Wars fan
would be at the Star Wars panel?"

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:39.880
Ah, life's crazy.

00:21:39.880 --> 00:21:41.340
[Audience member speaks
but is not near a microphone]

00:21:41.340 --> 00:21:42.500
Boyega: What?

00:21:42.500 --> 00:21:44.500
[Audience member speaks
but is not near a microphone]

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:47.640
Boyega: Oh, thank you man,
I appreciate that.

00:21:47.640 --> 00:21:49.120
Abrams: We're getting very complicated.
Boyega: Yeah, yeah.

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:52.420
Hardwick: It is. I know, that guy just
wants to have a conversation now.

00:21:52.420 --> 00:21:55.020
What are you doing later?
Boyega: Yeah. Hey, we'll talk later!

00:21:55.020 --> 00:21:57.020
Hardwick: Wanna get a taco?
What do you wanna do?

00:21:57.020 --> 00:21:59.020
Um --

00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:01.440
Daisy, I want to talk
about your tr--

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:03.140
Ridley: Woo!
Hardwick: Daisy, yes, please.

00:22:03.140 --> 00:22:05.140
[Audience clapping
and cheering]

00:22:06.340 --> 00:22:08.340
Let's talk about the physical demands --
Ridley: Yes.

00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:11.060
Hardwick: that you've had,
as compared to other stuff you've done.

00:22:11.060 --> 00:22:14.980
What was the training element like for you,
and did it change you?

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:17.420
Ridley: Firstly, we can't really compare it
to other stuff I've done,

00:22:17.420 --> 00:22:19.260
'cause I've not really done other stuff.

00:22:19.260 --> 00:22:22.720
Um -- it was, like -- I think,

00:22:22.720 --> 00:22:25.740
going into acting, you always hope
that physically, you can change yourself,

00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:27.680
as well as emotionally
embody a character.

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:32.700
So, to be able to have, like, solid months of training,
though difficult, was amazing.

00:22:32.700 --> 00:22:36.380
Like, our incredible stunt team --
oh my God, I've never seen those pictures.

00:22:36.380 --> 00:22:37.580
Um, uh,

00:22:37.580 --> 00:22:42.040
like the most incredible stunt team,
me and John got to work together a lot.

00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:44.180
And then, as you've seen the teaser:

00:22:44.180 --> 00:22:47.420
we were running through
125-degree heat.

00:22:47.420 --> 00:22:49.420
And those explosions were real.

00:22:49.420 --> 00:22:51.420
I mean, John, then, had it harder.

00:22:51.420 --> 00:22:55.500
I'd just like to thank J.J. Abrams
for filming in Abu Dhabi, in the desert,

00:22:55.500 --> 00:22:57.620
and making me wear
a stormtrooper outfit.

00:22:57.620 --> 00:22:58.960
[Hardwick et al laugh]

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:02.120
Boyega: Thank you, J.J.
You're honestly understanding.

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:03.540
[Audience laughs]

00:23:03.540 --> 00:23:06.020
Hardwick: It's great for all the fans, 
it's great for all the fans, 'cause they're like,

00:23:06.020 --> 00:23:07.920
"oh, he's really going there,
he's really shooting it!"

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:09.920
And the actors are like, "can we just do
like a green-screen thing? I mean, like..."

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:11.640
Boyega: Right, yeahh!

00:23:11.640 --> 00:23:16.180
Ridley: But yeah, it was amazing,
and I think the look of it, hopefully, pays off.

00:23:16.180 --> 00:23:19.260
And hopefully my dress reveals my guns, 
'cause I worked hard for these.

00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:21.000
[Hardwick et al laugh]
Isaac: Yes.

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:25.140
Hardwick: Oscar Isaac,
who's playing Poe Dameron.

00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:26.860
[Audience cheers]

00:23:26.860 --> 00:23:28.780
Isaac: Thank you.

00:23:28.780 --> 00:23:31.480
Hardwick: So, you said Poe was the --
I believe the quote was,

00:23:31.480 --> 00:23:33.760
"Best friggin' pilot in the galaxy."
Isaac: Yes.

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:36.740
Hardwick: Were you given
advice on your piloting?

00:23:36.740 --> 00:23:39.700
Did anything help you
become a better pilot?

00:23:39.700 --> 00:23:43.860
Isaac: Ah -- well yeah, you know,
as an excited, eager actor,

00:23:43.860 --> 00:23:46.940
you know, I wanted to talk
to Harrison about piloting,

00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:48.940
and about,

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:52.480
how should I do it, you know?
Is there any special things that I should do?

00:23:52.480 --> 00:23:56.380
Just, how do you work the controls?
And he said, um...

00:23:56.380 --> 00:23:59.360
"It's fake."
[Hardwick et al laugh]

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:03.500
"And also, it's in space,
so the same stuff doesn't really apply."

00:24:03.500 --> 00:24:06.900
[Hardwick chuckles]
Isaac: And I said, 'got it. I can do that.'

00:24:06.900 --> 00:24:10.880
But you know, I think really it's --
it was taking on,

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:14.080
you know, the character of Poe,
he takes on that legacy,

00:24:14.080 --> 00:24:19.160
not only of Han, but of Luke,
of being these heroic pilots.

00:24:19.160 --> 00:24:21.640
'Cause if you think about it,
these characters, ah,

00:24:21.640 --> 00:24:24.080
they lived those stories
that we all grew up with.

00:24:24.080 --> 00:24:27.400
They grew up at the same time
as those stories, you know? I mean...

00:24:27.400 --> 00:24:31.160
Poe was probably, he was there
watching on Yarvin, you know,

00:24:31.160 --> 00:24:33.000
when the medal ceremony
was happening,

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:35.280
thinking, "I want to be that.
I want to be a hero.

00:24:35.280 --> 00:24:37.280
And I want to take up that mantle."

00:24:37.280 --> 00:24:39.720
And, uh, I think that's
what he does, you know?

00:24:39.720 --> 00:24:42.220
He -- even sometimes 
recklessly so.

00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:46.300
Um, but uh -- so yeah,
really connecting it to that old world,

00:24:46.300 --> 00:24:48.120
it was special.

00:24:48.120 --> 00:24:52.040
Hardwick: Great! I want to give some of the fans
opportunity to ask you guys some questions.

00:24:52.040 --> 00:24:54.600
So let's go into the audience.

00:24:54.600 --> 00:24:56.360
Ask a couple questions.

00:24:56.360 --> 00:24:57.940
Here they come.

00:24:57.940 --> 00:24:59.580
Bounding up to the -- hello!

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:01.020
&gt;&gt; Hello there.
Hardwick: What is your name?

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:02.540
&gt;&gt; [Male speaker] My name is Ryan.
Hardwick: Hey, Ryan.

00:25:02.540 --> 00:25:04.780
&gt;&gt; [Female speaker] I'm Currin (?).
Hardwick: Hello, what's your question?

00:25:04.780 --> 00:25:06.480
&gt;&gt; [Male] This question is for J.J.

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:11.360
What we really like about your Star Trek films
is the diversity of the cast.

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:14.820
&gt;&gt; [Female] And we would like to know,
do you plan on having any Asian characters

00:25:14.820 --> 00:25:17.680
join the cast in any upcoming films?

00:25:17.680 --> 00:25:19.680
[Audience cheers]

00:25:21.420 --> 00:25:23.420
Abrams: Uh --

00:25:23.420 --> 00:25:25.420
First of all, I want to say, go Asians.

00:25:25.420 --> 00:25:27.640
[Laughter and cheering]
I mean --

00:25:27.640 --> 00:25:29.120
I think I --
Hardwick: 'S the right answer.

00:25:29.120 --> 00:25:30.680
Abrams: I think the whole room
feels that way.

00:25:30.680 --> 00:25:32.680
Uh -- uh --

00:25:32.680 --> 00:25:36.140
Here's the thing. I'm not casting
the movies that are coming up,

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:38.960
but if I were to, I would
cast them as only Asian.

00:25:38.960 --> 00:25:41.680
[Someone snorts with laughter]
[Hardwick belly-laughs]

00:25:41.680 --> 00:25:45.300
But, uh -- I think you'll be happy
to see, when you see this film,

00:25:45.300 --> 00:25:46.980
there are Asians in this film.

00:25:46.980 --> 00:25:49.860
So, I was involved
in the casting of this movie,

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:52.560
and, there -- 
but honestly, the truth is,

00:25:52.560 --> 00:25:56.000
we didn't write the character of Finn
to be, you know, any color.

00:25:56.000 --> 00:25:58.000
We didn't write the character 
of Rey to be any color.

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:00.820
We, you know -- 
we just cast this movie,

00:26:00.820 --> 00:26:03.520
knowing that it was important that we --
[Audience clapping]

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:06.920
We wanted the movie to look
the way the world looks.

00:26:06.920 --> 00:26:12.100
And, uh, I think it's important that people
see themselves represented in film.

00:26:12.100 --> 00:26:13.620
I think it's not a small thing.

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:18.080
So I completely understand, and think
your question is hugely important,

00:26:18.080 --> 00:26:20.080
and it was a big consideration.

00:26:20.080 --> 00:26:22.740
But I'm really proud of
the cast we ended up with,

00:26:22.740 --> 00:26:25.700
literally these actors
are extraordinary.

00:26:25.700 --> 00:26:28.060
And when you're working as long
and as hard as we all have on this movie,

00:26:28.060 --> 00:26:31.140
and you're in editing,
and you see the choices you have,

00:26:31.140 --> 00:26:33.400
in terms of performances,
take to take, uh,

00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:37.140
I could not be more grateful
to all you guys for the work you did.

00:26:37.140 --> 00:26:38.460
So, thanks for your question.

00:26:38.460 --> 00:26:42.540
Kennedy: And I will add to that --
I'll peek around the corner here and add to that,

00:26:42.540 --> 00:26:47.560
that there is every intention to carry on
exactly what J.J.'s talking about,

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:50.940
in all the Star Wars movies
that we're going to make.

00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:52.340
[Audience cheers and applauds]
Hardwick: Excellent.

00:26:52.340 --> 00:26:53.400
Abrams: Thank you.

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:55.700
Hardwick: Good tag-team question.
Yes, sir.

00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:58.260
&gt;&gt; Hi, Chris. My name is Monty Goulet.

00:26:58.260 --> 00:27:00.580
My question is for J.J.

00:27:00.580 --> 00:27:06.340
J.J., when you did the previous unreleased IPs
such as Lost and Fringe,

00:27:06.340 --> 00:27:08.040
you did a lot of transmedia.

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:13.220
And now you're doing transmedia for Star Wars,
one of the biggest IPs ever,

00:27:13.220 --> 00:27:16.020
with a big, expansive universe.

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:20.040
Is there anything you would do,
to take away from Star Wars,

00:27:20.040 --> 00:27:23.040
when you build a new IP
again in the future?

00:27:23.040 --> 00:27:25.480
Abrams: Uh --
Kennedy: Uh, don't answer that.

00:27:25.480 --> 00:27:27.300
[Kennedy et al laugh]

00:27:27.300 --> 00:27:29.140
Abrams: What Kathy doesn't
want me to say -- no.

00:27:29.140 --> 00:27:31.340
This is what I think.
Uh, and I --

00:27:31.340 --> 00:27:35.880
I'm interested that you use
the term "IP" so frequently.

00:27:35.880 --> 00:27:37.060
[Panelists chuckle]

00:27:37.060 --> 00:27:40.320
But I will say this:
I don't think of Star Wars as a --

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:42.240
I don't even know
how to answer that question.

00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:45.120
I appreciate it, but the answer
I could give you is...

00:27:45.120 --> 00:27:49.000
This is a story. And these are
characters that we love.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:55.300
And so, I'm -- none of us, I think, are thinking
about it from the outside in, like that.

00:27:55.300 --> 00:27:56.940
And it's weird, because
you come to something like this,

00:27:56.940 --> 00:27:59.020
and you want to 
make everyone happy,

00:27:59.020 --> 00:28:01.300
and you want to show something
that makes everyone excited.

00:28:01.300 --> 00:28:05.140
But the truth is, we're just working really hard
to make a movie and tell a story,

00:28:05.140 --> 00:28:09.520
and when things come up that we know
we need to do to sell it, and market it,

00:28:09.520 --> 00:28:12.360
we want that to be
as authentic as the piece itself.

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:16.120
So, I'm not quite sure
what the strategy would be.

00:28:16.120 --> 00:28:21.740
But I know that if we thought of it as an IP,
I think it would end up being a very different film.

00:28:21.740 --> 00:28:23.740
Hardwick: [chuckles] Mr. Goulet
suffers from lawyer syndrome.

00:28:23.740 --> 00:28:25.800
He can only talk about it in IP.

00:28:25.800 --> 00:28:27.940
Abrams: Monty hates me.
I'm so sorry. Ok thanks.

00:28:27.940 --> 00:28:29.520
Hardwick: Are we good?
You got a thumbs-up and a heart.

00:28:29.520 --> 00:28:30.800
Abrams: Thumbs-up.
Thumbs-up for Monty.

00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:32.460
Hardwick: Emoji heart.
Yes, what is your name, sir?

00:28:32.460 --> 00:28:34.960
&gt;&gt; Oh, hi. My name is Scott.

00:28:34.960 --> 00:28:40.860
And my question is for all of you guys,
depending on if you can answer this.

00:28:40.860 --> 00:28:43.400
[Ridley laughs]
&gt;&gt; Thanks. Um --

00:28:43.400 --> 00:28:47.380
Hardwick: "Can I live with you?"
[Panelists laugh]

00:28:47.380 --> 00:28:50.080
&gt;&gt; Anyways, um, 
my question is,

00:28:50.080 --> 00:28:56.720
what's it like to work with the original cast, 
the legends, say, like, Mark Hamill,

00:28:56.720 --> 00:28:59.860
Harrison Ford, and Carrie Fisher?

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:03.220
Hardwick: Yeah, let's throw that to the cast,
what it was like for you guys.

00:29:03.220 --> 00:29:04.460
Let's start with Oscar.

00:29:04.460 --> 00:29:07.620
Isaac: Uh, uh, um -- Harrison thought
I was wearing a wig.

00:29:07.620 --> 00:29:09.460
[Panelists laugh]

00:29:09.460 --> 00:29:11.740
So that was fun, that was cool.

00:29:11.740 --> 00:29:13.500
Hardwick: What about you, Daisy?

00:29:13.500 --> 00:29:16.400
Ridley: Um, it was --

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:20.100
Going from an outside fan,
going into filming Star Wars,

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:23.180
I think working with the legends
that have gone --

00:29:23.180 --> 00:29:26.360
have already made this kind of universe 
and are coming back to it,

00:29:26.360 --> 00:29:28.580
was everything we could have
hoped for, and more.

00:29:28.580 --> 00:29:31.560
They're funny and
warm and welcoming,

00:29:31.560 --> 00:29:37.680
and made us feel as part 
of the universe as they already are.

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:40.040
So it was great.
Hardwick: Excellent. John?

00:29:40.040 --> 00:29:41.540
Boyega: I have to say,
my greatest memory

00:29:41.540 --> 00:29:45.020
was taking Harrison Ford to a Nigerian
restaurant in Southeast London.

00:29:45.020 --> 00:29:46.000
[Ridley laughs]

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:49.580
And walking in and seeing all these Nigerians absolutely frozen at the sight of Harrison Ford.

00:29:49.580 --> 00:29:50.500
[Ridley laughs]

00:29:50.500 --> 00:29:53.380
And this guy comes up to him and says,
"Are you Harrison Ford?"

00:29:53.380 --> 00:29:55.500
Harrison Ford goes,
"I used to be."

00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:57.500
[Panelists laugh]
[Audience applauds]

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:02.340
Hardwick: Thank you very much
for your question.

00:30:02.340 --> 00:30:04.140
One more question for now.
Yes, what is your name?

00:30:04.140 --> 00:30:06.720
&gt;&gt; Yeah, my name's Mario, 
and this is for the whole panel,

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:08.580
including Chris.
Hardwick: What?

00:30:08.580 --> 00:30:12.620
&gt;&gt; Alright. If you guys were Jedis in real life,
what color would each of your lightsabers be?

00:30:12.620 --> 00:30:14.620
[Hardwick gasps]
[Audience claps]

00:30:16.640 --> 00:30:19.260
Kennedy: I'm gonna go
to blue, right away.

00:30:19.260 --> 00:30:21.640
Abrams: I have to -- I -- listen.
I'm gonna say red.

00:30:21.640 --> 00:30:23.180
Kennedy: Oh.
Abrams: I have to say it. Red.

00:30:23.180 --> 00:30:24.720
It would be. I know.

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:27.160
Kasdan: I would like to have
the first black --

00:30:27.160 --> 00:30:29.160
Ridley: Ooh!
Kennedy: Oh!

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:33.080
Boyega: I'd say, gold.

00:30:34.480 --> 00:30:36.480
Ridley: Then I'm gonna say silver.

00:30:37.580 --> 00:30:39.100
Isaac: Like, a rainbow-colored?

00:30:39.100 --> 00:30:40.800
[Panelists laugh]
Hardwick: Yeah. That's the best answer.

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:41.800
Boyega: Woo!

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:44.300
Hardwick: That was the best ans--
better than what I --

00:30:44.300 --> 00:30:46.300
Thank you.
Hey, and this is you.

00:30:46.300 --> 00:30:48.300
Um, alright, so,

00:30:48.300 --> 00:30:50.000
we've seen the Light side,

00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:53.560
but what you guys haven't seen yet 
is a little taste of the Dark side. So --

00:30:53.560 --> 00:30:56.420
[Audience cheers wildly]
[Isaac makes 'mwa-ha-ha' vocalization]

00:30:56.420 --> 00:30:57.880
Boyega: Hey, Daisy.

00:30:57.880 --> 00:31:00.840
Hardwick: I would like to 
bring out some people that, uh --

00:31:00.840 --> 00:31:04.000
let's call them 
relevant to your interests. Um --

00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:06.900
As Kylo Ren, please
welcome Adam Driver!

00:31:06.900 --> 00:31:08.900
[Audience clapping
and cheering]

00:31:14.840 --> 00:31:17.480
As General Hux,
Domhnall Gleeson!

00:31:17.480 --> 00:31:19.480
[Audience clapping
and cheering]

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:28.380
And as Captain Phasma,
Gwendoline Christie!

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:30.380
[Audience clapping
and cheering]

00:31:46.100 --> 00:31:49.780
Alright, so let's start
with Adam. Um --

00:31:49.780 --> 00:31:53.100
So, the images that we see, like,
we've seen some intense images,

00:31:53.100 --> 00:31:55.100
we've seen exciting images,

00:31:55.100 --> 00:32:00.540
can you tell us a little bit about 
your character's connection to the Dark side?

00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:05.280
Gwendoline Christie: Ah, well,
Captain Phasma is a First Order --

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:07.280
[Audience laughs]
I'm just speaking wildly.

00:32:07.280 --> 00:32:09.960
Hardwick: Let her go. Yeah.
Christie: This is so out of control, right now.

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:11.960
Hardwick: This is --
[Audience cheers loudly]

00:32:15.660 --> 00:32:19.020
Can I just say something, though?
You know you're on Game of Thrones, right?

00:32:19.020 --> 00:32:21.500
[Audience cheering continues]

00:32:21.500 --> 00:32:22.940
Christie: Game of who?

00:32:22.940 --> 00:32:25.360
Hardwick: [chuckles]
Ok. Nevermind.

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:27.760
Domhnall Gleeson: You're on
Game of Thrones. That's amazing.

00:32:27.760 --> 00:32:31.840
Hardwick: Adam. What can you tell us
about your connection to the Dark side?

00:32:31.840 --> 00:32:34.140
Can you tell us anything?

00:32:34.140 --> 00:32:35.620
Adam Driver: No.
Hardwick: Ok.

00:32:35.620 --> 00:32:38.200
[Panelists and audience laugh]

00:32:38.200 --> 00:32:40.200
Now, Domhnall, the --
[Panelists laugh]

00:32:42.740 --> 00:32:45.680
There is a -- a sort of an unspoken tradition

00:32:45.680 --> 00:32:49.320
uh -- in the original Star Wars, 
of bad guys being these, sort of,

00:32:49.320 --> 00:32:51.900
ehh, let's call them 
'humorless British types,' and kind of --

00:32:51.900 --> 00:32:55.080
Gleeson: Are you really moving on to me?
And you're letting him get away with that?

00:32:55.080 --> 00:32:57.800
Hardwick: Well, I can't -- he's not --
there's nothing he can say. Yeah. That was it.

00:32:57.800 --> 00:33:00.320
Isaac: Like, come on,  you gotta say something. 
Adam, say something.

00:33:00.320 --> 00:33:03.540
Driver: Uh, I, um...
what do you want me to say?

00:33:03.540 --> 00:33:05.680
[Panelists and audience chuckling]

00:33:05.680 --> 00:33:07.860
Hardwick: Who are you?
Driver: That's a good question.

00:33:07.860 --> 00:33:10.080
Hardwick: Yeah, see, he doesn't even --

00:33:10.080 --> 00:33:12.780
Driver: Good question.
Hardwick: Can you tell us anything about --

00:33:12.780 --> 00:33:16.180
Gleeson: Oh, man.
Driver: I mean --

00:33:16.180 --> 00:33:18.600
I mean, what can I say?

00:33:18.600 --> 00:33:22.660
We didn't really have a lot 
of conversations about, um,

00:33:22.660 --> 00:33:27.360
bad or evil, actually,
 when we were shooting it.

00:33:27.360 --> 00:33:37.080
It was more, 'what do you think is
the difference between being bad and being right?'

00:33:37.080 --> 00:33:39.220
I think, which is 
a huge difference.

00:33:39.220 --> 00:33:42.500
Someone who thinks they're bad 
is almost kind of like a selfish thing.

00:33:42.500 --> 00:33:46.700
And it's mostly about them, and hurting 
somebody else, to get what they want.

00:33:46.700 --> 00:33:50.020
But thinking they're right 
is something different. Uh --

00:33:50.020 --> 00:33:53.000
That's almost -- more evil.

00:33:53.000 --> 00:33:56.760
A group of people who 
have decided that, um --

00:33:56.760 --> 00:33:58.760
[Trails off]

00:33:58.760 --> 00:34:00.300
[Gleeson and Christie chuckle]

00:34:00.300 --> 00:34:02.740
That they're morally justified 
in behaving a certain way.

00:34:02.740 --> 00:34:04.900
And I think that's what we,
kind of, approach to them. [sic]

00:34:04.900 --> 00:34:06.900
And less like, um, a 'bad' person.

00:34:06.900 --> 00:34:09.540
Hardwick: Sure, 'cause no character 
would really go, "I am evil."

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:13.180
They just want what they want, and that 
doesn't always jive with what everyone else wants.

00:34:13.180 --> 00:34:14.780
Driver: Right, yeah.
Hardwick: Yeah. Perfect.

00:34:14.780 --> 00:34:17.220
Gleeson: [indistinct] in the film, though.
I am evil.

00:34:17.220 --> 00:34:18.860
[Laughter]

00:34:18.860 --> 00:34:20.780
All the times.
Hardwick: Huge spoiler.

00:34:20.780 --> 00:34:23.660
Well, Domhnall, what can you tell us 
about your character?

00:34:23.660 --> 00:34:25.480
Gleeson: I am evil.
Hardwick: Ok!

00:34:25.480 --> 00:34:26.700
[Laughter]

00:34:26.700 --> 00:34:28.700
Are you, though? Really?
Is your character flat-out evil?

00:34:28.700 --> 00:34:31.080
Gleeson: Uhhm... 
He's British, so, yeah.

00:34:31.080 --> 00:34:33.080
[Laughter]

00:34:36.620 --> 00:34:42.240
Hardwick: He's using a large
weaponized machine to obtain something.

00:34:42.240 --> 00:34:44.480
Gleeson: Yeahh... 
just like the British. Yeah.

00:34:44.480 --> 00:34:46.480
[Laughter]

00:34:46.480 --> 00:34:49.000
Yep. Ahh --

00:34:49.000 --> 00:34:53.740
Hardwick: So, Gwendoline, 
you are covered in, head to toe, in armor,

00:34:53.740 --> 00:34:56.800
which I feel like 
is very familiar to you.

00:34:56.800 --> 00:34:59.460
How do you -- you know,
does that get in the way

00:34:59.460 --> 00:35:01.120
when you're trying to 
portray a character,

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:05.180
and you have this kind of cumbersome 
stuff that you have to work around?

00:35:05.180 --> 00:35:07.180
Or do you work it 
into the character?

00:35:07.180 --> 00:35:09.580
Christie: I found it exciting.

00:35:09.580 --> 00:35:14.860
Because, I found it not only exciting 
that there was a female stormtrooper,

00:35:14.860 --> 00:35:16.860
[Audience cheering loudly]

00:35:19.020 --> 00:35:24.080
But it was the opportunity 
to explore a female character

00:35:24.080 --> 00:35:28.720
that is totally not about 
the way she looks in flesh.

00:35:28.720 --> 00:35:34.780
And that armor is exterior, 
and it's more like the outside feeding in.

00:35:34.780 --> 00:35:38.920
And I just found it very exciting 
that underneath that armor is a woman.

00:35:38.920 --> 00:35:42.080
And I think it makes it really 
more relevant than ever.

00:35:42.080 --> 00:35:44.080
Hardwick: Excellent.
[Audience cheering]

00:35:46.620 --> 00:35:48.940
I'm sure you have a couple 
of questions for the Dark side,

00:35:48.940 --> 00:35:54.080
so let's, uh, let's turn on the light 
and let a couple people ask some questions.

00:35:54.080 --> 00:35:56.080
Lights!
Oh, I'm magic.

00:35:56.080 --> 00:35:58.080
Yes, you, sir.

00:35:58.080 --> 00:36:01.480
&gt;&gt; Hello, it's good 
to meet the bad guys. Um --

00:36:01.480 --> 00:36:04.380
I wanted to know, because 
I was at Star Wars Celebration,

00:36:04.380 --> 00:36:05.300
[Scattered audience applause]

00:36:05.300 --> 00:36:08.240
and I got to hear John 
and Daisy and Oscar's story about

00:36:08.240 --> 00:36:10.240
their reaction when 
they were cast in Star Wars,

00:36:10.240 --> 00:36:13.340
so I'd like to hear 
the three new people --

00:36:13.340 --> 00:36:16.700
what was your reaction when you found out 
you were going to be in Star Wars?

00:36:18.340 --> 00:36:19.920
Driver: Domhnall.

00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:21.820
Gleeson: Uh, that's 
what you said, really?

00:36:21.820 --> 00:36:24.060
[Driver chuckles]
Gleeson: Uh -- no, uhm,

00:36:24.060 --> 00:36:27.360
it was -- I met 
with J.J. and, uh,

00:36:27.360 --> 00:36:29.060
Mr. Lawrence Kasdan,

00:36:29.060 --> 00:36:33.680
and then they told me this -- 
the story and who the guy was,

00:36:33.680 --> 00:36:37.080
ehm, and that he was 
in charge of people,

00:36:37.080 --> 00:36:40.380
eh, on a star, like Starkiller Base, 
sort of thing.

00:36:40.380 --> 00:36:43.200
And -- uh-oh, hang on.
Is that alright?

00:36:43.200 --> 00:36:44.540
Yeah, yeah, that's fine.
Abrams: That's ok.

00:36:44.540 --> 00:36:46.080
Gleeson: Em --
[Audience laughter]

00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:47.480
Hardwick: Do you wanna talk 
about that for a second?

00:36:47.480 --> 00:36:49.040
Gleeson: Uh, no, I don't want 
to talk about it.

00:36:49.040 --> 00:36:50.400
Unidentifiable male panelist:
What a jerk.

00:36:50.400 --> 00:36:54.900
Abrams: Yeah, we -- the name 
of the base of the First Order

00:36:54.900 --> 00:36:58.880
is in honor of the original 
last name of Luke Skywalker,

00:36:58.880 --> 00:37:00.620
is Starkiller Base.

00:37:00.620 --> 00:37:03.040
Spoiled by Domhnall Gleeson.

00:37:03.040 --> 00:37:05.860
[Laughter]
[Audience clapping]

00:37:05.860 --> 00:37:09.360
Gleeson: What else do you want to know?
What else do you want to know?

00:37:09.360 --> 00:37:12.240
Hardwick: You can't fire him now, 
so it's all in fun.

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:14.460
Already done. Yeah.

00:37:14.460 --> 00:37:17.100
Uh, what about you?
What about you, Gwendoline?

00:37:17.100 --> 00:37:20.560
Christie: I just didn't think 
it could possibly really happen.

00:37:20.560 --> 00:37:24.680
I thought that -- when I heard 
it was sort of happening,

00:37:24.680 --> 00:37:28.320
and going for fittings 
and things, I still thought,

00:37:28.320 --> 00:37:32.540
"there's every chance that they can 
just replace me with someone else."

00:37:32.540 --> 00:37:37.480
And every day was the most surreal, 
bizarre experience, of --

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:39.720
well, you know, 
like it is for all of you.

00:37:39.720 --> 00:37:43.000
It's in such a part 
of the fabric of your being,

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:45.700
and then you're suddenly involved in it.

00:37:45.700 --> 00:37:47.480
Hardwick: No, I don't know
what it's like to be in Star Wars.

00:37:47.480 --> 00:37:49.620
[Christie et al laugh]

00:37:49.620 --> 00:37:52.140
Christie: Actually, I was just 
in the car, coming here,

00:37:52.140 --> 00:37:55.840
and kind of realized then, and thought,
"Oh, I should have thought of some things to say."

00:37:55.840 --> 00:37:58.900
Hardwick: Yeah. Uh,
hello, what is your name, sir?

00:38:00.200 --> 00:38:02.200
&gt;&gt; Hi, my  name is Jonathan.

00:38:02.200 --> 00:38:04.000
Hardwick: And what is 
your question, Jonathan?

00:38:04.000 --> 00:38:08.000
&gt;&gt; Is there going to be any mention 
of Darth Vegas [sic: for Plagueis?]

00:38:08.000 --> 00:38:13.640
in Episode VII? Maybe surrounding 
a certain staff, that someone finds?

00:38:13.640 --> 00:38:16.660
[Kennedy tsks, and 
over-dramatically sighs]

00:38:16.660 --> 00:38:17.600
Kenney: Is that --

00:38:17.600 --> 00:38:20.120
Abrams: Larry? A-hem.
Do you want to answer that?

00:38:20.120 --> 00:38:25.920
Kasdan: Uh -- I don't think I heard correctly.
Is it Darth Vegas? Like, uh, Las Vegas?

00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:27.340
[Ridley laughs]

00:38:27.340 --> 00:38:29.640
Kasdan: Darth F--

00:38:29.640 --> 00:38:31.180
Vader?
Abrams: No, he's --

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:33.180
Larry's messing with you. Uh --

00:38:33.180 --> 00:38:37.520
I don't think we -- we don't want to be 
talking about story too much, too soon.

00:38:37.520 --> 00:38:39.080
But, uh --

00:38:39.080 --> 00:38:41.080
I will say, no.

00:38:41.080 --> 00:38:42.880
[Chuckling and laughter]

00:38:42.880 --> 00:38:44.620
Hardwick: Great, let's take - 
Abrams: Thank you. Thank you.

00:38:44.620 --> 00:38:47.000
Hardwick: You're welcome.
Let's take one more question.

00:38:47.000 --> 00:38:48.240
Hey.

00:38:48.240 --> 00:38:50.000
&gt;&gt; Hey, how's it going again?

00:38:50.000 --> 00:38:52.860
Hardwick: Pretty sweet.
Riley: Good! [Laughs]

00:38:52.860 --> 00:38:54.720
Hardwick: How are you?
&gt;&gt; Good. Uh,

00:38:54.720 --> 00:38:57.180
Gwendoline, I just wanna say, 
you're a goddess.

00:38:57.180 --> 00:38:59.180
I think you already knew that.
[Audience cheering]

00:38:59.180 --> 00:39:00.360
Riley: Yes.

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:04.440
&gt;&gt; Short and sweet.
Uh, cast, um, just wanna know,

00:39:04.440 --> 00:39:08.820
are you part of the Light side 
or the Dark side of the Force?

00:39:08.820 --> 00:39:10.820
Hardwick: You mean, in real life,
you want to know?

00:39:10.820 --> 00:39:13.720
&gt;&gt; I just want to know,
which is their preference.

00:39:13.720 --> 00:39:15.540
If they had to choose --
Riley: Ohh.

00:39:15.540 --> 00:39:18.540
Hardwick: Ok. Let's start with John.
One word answer.

00:39:18.540 --> 00:39:21.860
Boyega: Oh, I wouldn't be involved in any of that. 
I would be Han Solo, trying to make some money.

00:39:21.860 --> 00:39:23.860
Hardwick: Yeah.
[Riley et al laugh]

00:39:23.860 --> 00:39:26.660
[Audience applause]

00:39:26.660 --> 00:39:29.620
Another chaotic good.
Riley: And I'd be his sidekick.

00:39:29.620 --> 00:39:32.180
[Chuckles]
Hardwick: Oscar.

00:39:32.180 --> 00:39:34.700
Isaac: I'll say Light side.
Light side of the Force.

00:39:34.700 --> 00:39:36.700
Hardwick: Adam.
Driver: Dark. Dark. Dark.

00:39:36.700 --> 00:39:38.700
[Audience chuckles]
Gleeson: Yeah, me too.

00:39:38.700 --> 00:39:40.700
Christie: I like to go Dark.

00:39:40.700 --> 00:39:42.700
Hardwick: [chuckles]
Excellent.

00:39:44.020 --> 00:39:47.260
Uh, we're just gonna
suspend questions for a second.

00:39:47.260 --> 00:39:49.700
Because at this point, I, uh --

00:39:49.700 --> 00:39:52.200
I don't know, I thought it might 
be kind of fun to, uh --

00:39:52.200 --> 00:39:53.860
bring out Carrie Fisher!

00:39:53.860 --> 00:39:55.860
[Audience clapping
and cheering]

00:40:11.220 --> 00:40:13.600
Carrie Fisher: You came! Oh!

00:40:15.860 --> 00:40:18.980
Ok. What are you doing here?

00:40:18.980 --> 00:40:21.560
What are the odds?
All right.

00:40:21.560 --> 00:40:22.700
[Isaac chuckles]

00:40:22.700 --> 00:40:24.080
Hardwick: Hi, Carrie Fisher!

00:40:24.080 --> 00:40:25.720
Fisher: [overdramatic sigh]
Oh, hi.

00:40:25.720 --> 00:40:27.720
[Hardwick et al laugh]

00:40:27.720 --> 00:40:29.360
[Audience member 
yells something indistinct]

00:40:29.360 --> 00:40:32.080
Hi, my darling, 
how are you?

00:40:32.080 --> 00:40:34.680
Love the hair. Ok.

00:40:34.680 --> 00:40:38.220
Hardwick: So, can you 
give us a little --

00:40:38.220 --> 00:40:40.980
I'm sure you've been answering this question a lot, 
but, we're all here, and --

00:40:40.980 --> 00:40:42.980
Fisher: did I know it was going 
to be that big of a hit?

00:40:42.980 --> 00:40:46.040
Hardwick: [chuckles]
Well, yes, I was thinking that,

00:40:46.040 --> 00:40:50.400
but I was afraid to ask it.
But, so my other question, uh,

00:40:50.400 --> 00:40:53.520
is, what was the feeling like for you,

00:40:53.520 --> 00:40:56.000
when you walked on the set, 
and you saw this all come to life again?

00:40:56.000 --> 00:40:59.140
I mean, I know you've lived 
with it for a long time, but,

00:40:59.140 --> 00:41:03.840
to see it so tangibly, and to be a part 
of it again, what was that feeling like?

00:41:03.840 --> 00:41:08.740
Fisher: It was like a flashback.
Kind of. You know, I thought --

00:41:08.740 --> 00:41:11.780
They were right about 
the, uh, acid flashbacks.

00:41:11.780 --> 00:41:14.760
[Laughter]
No, uh --

00:41:14.760 --> 00:41:18.480
I didn't think that it was 
going to happen again.

00:41:18.480 --> 00:41:21.460
I had to check and see if, 
you know, it was just me.

00:41:21.460 --> 00:41:24.360
Is, my voice,
coming out of my mouth.

00:41:24.360 --> 00:41:28.960
Uh -- and no, it was --
we just -- I always said, it was...

00:41:28.960 --> 00:41:31.680
a little bit like before, 
only we looked

00:41:31.680 --> 00:41:33.680
more melted, this time.

00:41:33.680 --> 00:41:35.680
[Laughter]

00:41:35.680 --> 00:41:40.580
But in a good way.
That kind of Force-melted.

00:41:40.580 --> 00:41:45.120
Hardwick: And did it feel like 
you just picked up where you left off before?

00:41:45.120 --> 00:41:48.200
Or did it feel like
a new experience?

00:41:48.200 --> 00:41:51.140
Fisher: Well, sort of like 
we picked up where we left off,

00:41:51.140 --> 00:41:53.840
but we left off a while ago.

00:41:53.840 --> 00:41:58.140
So, it was a long leave-off,
and then we're back.

00:41:58.140 --> 00:42:02.000
Thought, there were certain times, 
when the three of us were standing together, and,

00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:07.580
it was hard to, sort of, be there 
and then observe an observer.

00:42:07.580 --> 00:42:12.020
So -- but we're the legacy players.
We're known as the legacy people.

00:42:12.020 --> 00:42:15.820
So I always think of it as kind of like 
a tap dancing troupe, or something like that.

00:42:15.820 --> 00:42:17.960
[Audience laughter]
But it was great.

00:42:17.960 --> 00:42:20.500
And it was great 
having the new people.

00:42:20.500 --> 00:42:22.500
'Cause they do it faster.

00:42:22.500 --> 00:42:25.480
[Panelists and audience laugh]

00:42:25.480 --> 00:42:27.980
Hardwick: Well, you know, 
speaking of legacy people,

00:42:27.980 --> 00:42:29.980
maybe I should 
bring out Mark Hamill!

00:42:29.980 --> 00:42:31.980
[Audience cheering and clapping]

00:42:43.280 --> 00:42:46.360
Mark Hamill: Hello, everyone.
Hello, hello.

00:42:48.020 --> 00:42:49.560
Hardwick: Mr. Hamill.
Hamil: Yes, yes.

00:42:49.560 --> 00:42:53.280
We have a picture 
of you from 1976,

00:42:53.280 --> 00:42:57.380
which I'm sure people have seen.
Can we throw that up there?

00:42:57.380 --> 00:42:59.180
There's you from 1976.
Riley: Hey!

00:42:59.180 --> 00:43:01.180
Hardwick: With the original --

00:43:01.180 --> 00:43:02.960
I have that shirt. It's an original 
Ralph McQuarrie design.

00:43:02.960 --> 00:43:05.000
And I have -- I -- 
I have one of those.

00:43:05.000 --> 00:43:06.420
Uh -- and --
[Audience chuckles]

00:43:06.420 --> 00:43:09.500
So this was before Star Wars came out, 
and you were at a fan convention,

00:43:09.500 --> 00:43:11.820
because that was actually 
a part of your life before.

00:43:11.820 --> 00:43:14.380
What are you telling people 
about Star Wars before it's come out?

00:43:14.380 --> 00:43:17.560
Are you like, "I'm in a space movie.
I hope you see it." Like, what do you say?

00:43:17.560 --> 00:43:19.100
At that point?

00:43:19.100 --> 00:43:22.500
Hamill: Well, we only had photographs, 
there was no footage.

00:43:22.500 --> 00:43:25.980
And I went with Charlie Lippincott, 
and I think it was in Kansas.

00:43:25.980 --> 00:43:28.980
We had R2-D2 as a prop,

00:43:28.980 --> 00:43:33.860
we had C-3P0, although 
Anthony Daniels wasn't inside the costume.

00:43:33.860 --> 00:43:40.560
But as you can imagine, with only 25 photographs 
of various scenes in the movie,

00:43:40.560 --> 00:43:45.820
it wasn't really easy to describe
what it was.

00:43:45.820 --> 00:43:48.740
Um -- but --

00:43:48.740 --> 00:43:52.500
My enthusiasm for it 
was just off the charts.

00:43:52.500 --> 00:43:54.820
And, uh, uh --

00:43:54.820 --> 00:44:02.100
I'd been a fan -- I think my first convention 
I'd ever been to was at the Ambassador Hotel in 1972.

00:44:02.100 --> 00:44:08.460
So I was, you know, I'd been a fan since I'd caught 
the black and white King Kong on television,

00:44:08.460 --> 00:44:10.580
when I was 5 or 6 years old.

00:44:10.580 --> 00:44:15.420
And I think the fans understand that. 
I mean, you know, I'm one of them.

00:44:15.420 --> 00:44:21.640
And they're -- obviously, over my career, 
they've been a really important part of my life,

00:44:21.640 --> 00:44:25.020
and, you know, 'cause without you guys,
we're nothing.

00:44:26.520 --> 00:44:28.940
[Audience clapping
and cheering]

00:44:28.940 --> 00:44:31.600
Hardwick: Is there anything --
I mean, this is --

00:44:31.600 --> 00:44:34.740
This is a very momentous occasion,
having you guys together on stage like this.

00:44:34.740 --> 00:44:37.520
I mean, is there anything, a message, 
that you have for the fans

00:44:37.520 --> 00:44:42.820
who've been on this journey, since 1976?
Is there anything to take away from it?

00:44:45.560 --> 00:44:48.960
Hamill: Wow. [Chuckles] Um...

00:44:51.160 --> 00:44:55.100
I -- I just -- like I say, the --

00:44:55.100 --> 00:44:58.420
Well, keep coming back, yes.
But, no, uh --

00:44:58.420 --> 00:45:00.640
You know, it's hard to describe,

00:45:00.640 --> 00:45:03.980
because when I meet you on the street, 
everyone has a story.

00:45:03.980 --> 00:45:06.960
"I met my wife 
at the premiere of Jedi,"

00:45:06.960 --> 00:45:10.140
or, "my son is named Luke."
Or whatever it is.

00:45:10.140 --> 00:45:12.400
It's such a personal connection.

00:45:12.400 --> 00:45:14.880
And it's very moving to me.

00:45:14.880 --> 00:45:18.800
It's sometimes hard to absorb 
the impact you could have .

00:45:18.800 --> 00:45:21.780
It's almost an out-of-body experience.

00:45:21.780 --> 00:45:25.880
Uh, you know, because, uh,

00:45:25.880 --> 00:45:30.440
it's -- I see it put together,
it's not me, it's Luke.

00:45:30.440 --> 00:45:33.920
But, uh, I've never 
taken it for granted.

00:45:33.920 --> 00:45:40.220
And like I say, it's -- 
I'm moved by the connection to the world.

00:45:40.220 --> 00:45:46.180
I mean, I've suddenly become a -- 
your friend, to everyone.

00:45:46.180 --> 00:45:49.320
I mean, my wife and I went 
to Tahiti on our honeymoon,

00:45:49.320 --> 00:45:54.020
thinking, "There's nobody, 
gonna know about this movie,

00:45:54.020 --> 00:45:57.700
"way out in this paradise."

00:45:57.700 --> 00:46:01.420
And we're on the back porch 
of the little hut, on the water.

00:46:01.420 --> 00:46:06.580
And I saw a motor boat coming towards 
the back of -- coming towards us.

00:46:06.580 --> 00:46:09.580
And as it got closer and closer and closer, 
I thought I was losing my mind,

00:46:09.580 --> 00:46:12.940
because at the helm,

00:46:12.940 --> 00:46:14.780
was a guy 
in a Darth Vader mask.

00:46:14.780 --> 00:46:17.080
[Ridley et al laugh]

00:46:17.080 --> 00:46:18.480
In Tahiti!

00:46:18.480 --> 00:46:22.800
Turns out somebody bought it 
in Hawaii and came to Tahiti,

00:46:22.800 --> 00:46:27.020
they were filming a movie, 
Dino De Laurentiis was making Hurricane,

00:46:27.020 --> 00:46:30.500
and the accountant on the film 
had done Star Wars.

00:46:30.500 --> 00:46:32.500
He said, "You know 
what would really surprise Mark?

00:46:32.500 --> 00:46:35.140
"Drive up to his hut 
in your Darth Vader mask!"

00:46:35.140 --> 00:46:36.660
[Laughter]
So, uh --

00:46:36.660 --> 00:46:39.160
Fisher: No, but it's true.
When you hear people that --

00:46:39.160 --> 00:46:40.980
They come and 
they say, you know,

00:46:40.980 --> 00:46:44.920
"I waited to show my daughter.
I wanted to make sure I showed my kids --"

00:46:44.920 --> 00:46:47.340
That it was something 
that you can do as a family.

00:46:47.340 --> 00:46:48.300
Hamill: Yeah.

00:46:48.300 --> 00:46:50.780
Fisher: You want to make sure 
that you show them at the right time,

00:46:50.780 --> 00:46:52.780
and then you watch them, like,

00:46:52.780 --> 00:46:56.400
"if we don't like the same character,
I'm not gonna like my kid!"

00:46:56.400 --> 00:46:58.400
[Laughter]

00:47:00.180 --> 00:47:03.000
Hamill: Yeah, it's a generational 
thing now, so, you know,

00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:05.960
my son was born when 
we were doing Empire Strikes Back,

00:47:05.960 --> 00:47:07.520
now he's, uh,

00:47:07.520 --> 00:47:09.480
he's the real Star Wars expert 
in my family.

00:47:09.480 --> 00:47:14.600
I hate to tell you guys. I did 
a Star Wars trivia contest, er, quiz,

00:47:14.600 --> 00:47:16.460
and flunked.
[Audience laughter]

00:47:16.460 --> 00:47:19.420
You know, I said, "I can't remember
 what Han Solo was smuggling.

00:47:19.420 --> 00:47:22.660
"Was it jewels? Or -- ?"
No, it was spices.

00:47:22.660 --> 00:47:24.760
You know, you guys, now --
I think, ownership,

00:47:24.760 --> 00:47:27.840
I've ceded ownership 
to the world at large.

00:47:27.840 --> 00:47:31.040
[Hardwick et al laughter]
[Audience laughter]

00:47:31.040 --> 00:47:32.420
Years ago.

00:47:32.420 --> 00:47:34.420
Fisher: I said
it wasn't spices.

00:47:34.420 --> 00:47:36.420
[Laughter]

00:47:37.860 --> 00:47:39.860
Hardwick: You crazy siblings.
Hamill: I don't want to know.

00:47:39.860 --> 00:47:44.480
Hardwick: Uh --
J.J., how -- just, since --

00:47:44.480 --> 00:47:47.860
There -- how -- 
Is Harrison ok? Has be been ok?

00:47:47.860 --> 00:47:49.860
Abrams: Uh, well
why don't we see for ourselves!

00:47:49.860 --> 00:47:52.560
[Audience cheering
and clapping]

00:47:52.560 --> 00:47:54.560
Hardwick: Ladies and gentlemen,
Harrison Ford!

00:48:23.580 --> 00:48:26.300
Harrison Ford:
Thank you. Thank you.

00:48:26.300 --> 00:48:29.560
Hardwick: Ah, I'm sorry,
I really have to do this. I apologize.

00:48:29.560 --> 00:48:32.660
[Ridley et al laugh]
I made them all take a picture back stage,

00:48:32.660 --> 00:48:38.020
and I took a selfie. And I completely
blocked Harrison Ford in the picture, like an ass.

00:48:38.020 --> 00:48:41.600
So if everyone could just 
lean in a little bit, uh --

00:48:41.600 --> 00:48:43.600
Thank you, got 'em.
Alright. Um --

00:48:45.300 --> 00:48:47.760
First of all, thank you 
for coming out, Harrison Ford.

00:48:47.760 --> 00:48:49.360
[Audience cheering]

00:48:49.360 --> 00:48:50.840
Fisher: Thank you.

00:48:50.840 --> 00:48:52.840
[Cheering continues]

00:49:02.920 --> 00:49:05.960
Hardwick: How are you feeling?
Are you feeling good? Are you feeling ok?

00:49:05.960 --> 00:49:07.780
Ford: Yeah, I'm fine.
Hardwick: Good!

00:49:07.780 --> 00:49:09.480
Ford: How are you?
Hardwick: I'm ok!

00:49:09.480 --> 00:49:11.120
Ford: Good! Good --
Hardwick: I'm at a Star Wars panel.

00:49:11.120 --> 00:49:15.500
Ford: I'm great. Yeah. I'm fine. 
Thank you very much. Great.

00:49:15.500 --> 00:49:18.240
Hardwick: They're asking 
about your foot.

00:49:18.240 --> 00:49:21.020
Ford: My foot. My foot, uh,
just walked here --

00:49:21.020 --> 00:49:23.520
Hardwick: Yeah, he seems fine.
[Audience clapping]

00:49:23.520 --> 00:49:25.520
Not just walked, 
he bounded up the stairs.

00:49:25.520 --> 00:49:27.360
Ford: How bad could it be? 
Yeah, no, I'm good.

00:49:27.360 --> 00:49:29.840
I really appreciate it, 
thank you. Thanks for asking.

00:49:29.840 --> 00:49:32.480
Hardwick: Yeah, of course. Good.
Ford: Thank you for asking.

00:49:32.480 --> 00:49:36.160
Hardwick: What -- from you point of view, 
walking back onto the set,

00:49:36.160 --> 00:49:37.600
working with J.J.,
seeing the Millenium Fal --

00:49:37.600 --> 00:49:39.420
like, what did all that 
feel like, again?

00:49:39.420 --> 00:49:42.260
Ford: [Chuckles]
Well, it should have felt ridiculous.

00:49:42.260 --> 00:49:44.820
Uh --
[Audience laughter]

00:49:44.820 --> 00:49:48.780
It was thirty -- you know,
it was thirty-[growly noise] years ago.

00:49:48.780 --> 00:49:53.740
And -- and I --
sort of grew up.

00:49:53.740 --> 00:49:55.540
And, uh -- 
[Audience chuckling]

00:49:55.540 --> 00:50:00.000
And yet here I was, 
doing something I did so long ago,

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:01.720
and...

00:50:01.720 --> 00:50:04.380
I will tell you 
that it felt great.

00:50:04.380 --> 00:50:06.380
It felt --
[Audience cheering]

00:50:10.920 --> 00:50:14.220
I wasn't so sure 
it would, but, uh --

00:50:14.220 --> 00:50:18.480
the company
was the right company.

00:50:18.480 --> 00:50:21.740
The director was 
the right director.

00:50:21.740 --> 00:50:25.120
Larry wrote us 
a wonderful story.

00:50:25.120 --> 00:50:29.780
And I was proud and grateful 
to, once again, be involved.

00:50:29.780 --> 00:50:34.040
You know --
[chuckles, clears throat]

00:50:34.040 --> 00:50:39.300
This -- the original Star Wars 
that I was a part of,

00:50:39.300 --> 00:50:44.900
really was the beginning 
of my working life.

00:50:44.900 --> 00:50:46.020
And, uh --

00:50:46.020 --> 00:50:50.340
I was very, very grateful for 
the opportunity that I had in that film,

00:50:50.340 --> 00:50:53.020
and for the success 
of that film.

00:50:53.020 --> 00:50:56.800
And, uh -- so it was great
to be back. Thank you.

00:50:56.800 --> 00:50:58.800
Hardwick: Of course. I mean --
[Audience clapping and cheering]

00:51:00.780 --> 00:51:04.340
I don't think there was --

00:51:04.340 --> 00:51:07.020
I don't think there was 
one person that I knew,

00:51:07.020 --> 00:51:11.240
when we saw that other footage 
that you released, not that long ago,

00:51:11.240 --> 00:51:12.940
and you do the, 
"We're home,"

00:51:12.940 --> 00:51:14.720
and, like, every dude 
that I knew, I called, like,

00:51:14.720 --> 00:51:18.340
"Did you cry? I cried. Did you cry, too? You cried. 
I cried. I watched three times, I cried three times."

00:51:18.340 --> 00:51:19.580
[Ridley et al chuckle]

00:51:19.580 --> 00:51:22.280
So, we all --

00:51:22.280 --> 00:51:25.520
We all are a part 
of this as well,

00:51:25.520 --> 00:51:29.380
and it's so meaningful that 
the three of you agreed to be in this,

00:51:29.380 --> 00:51:31.560
and really kind of 
bring us home.

00:51:31.560 --> 00:51:34.300
All together, bring us home,
in a weird sort of way.

00:51:34.300 --> 00:51:36.040
[Audience clapping
and cheering]

00:51:36.040 --> 00:51:38.900
So --
[Clapping and cheering continue]

00:51:38.900 --> 00:51:40.460
Ah --

00:51:40.460 --> 00:51:43.020
I want to, now that everyone 
is out on the panel,

00:51:43.020 --> 00:51:45.900
I would love to throw it to the audience
to ask a couple of questions,

00:51:45.900 --> 00:51:47.900
as I'm sure you're eager to do.

00:51:47.900 --> 00:51:49.900
So, let's start with you, sir.
Hi, what's your name?

00:51:49.900 --> 00:51:51.440
&gt;&gt; Hello, my name is Bradley.
How are you?

00:51:51.440 --> 00:51:53.320
Hardwick: Hey, Bradley!
Make sure and lean in to the microphone.

00:51:53.320 --> 00:51:54.640
&gt;&gt; Will do.
Hardwick: Ok.

00:51:54.640 --> 00:51:57.080
&gt;&gt; So this is a question 
for the returning cast,

00:51:57.080 --> 00:52:01.300
but if any other cast or crew want to 
answer as well, feel free to do so.

00:52:01.300 --> 00:52:05.620
What stands out to you as the biggest difference 
in terms of theme in this new movie

00:52:05.620 --> 00:52:07.620
compared to the original trilogy?

00:52:09.760 --> 00:52:13.200
Ford: I don't know 
that it's a difference in theme.

00:52:13.200 --> 00:52:16.000
It's a development of theme.

00:52:16.000 --> 00:52:21.400
And it's a natural 
progression of the --

00:52:21.400 --> 00:52:26.920
uh -- that has occurred from 
the stories that we told in the first three.

00:52:26.920 --> 00:52:29.700
And, uh --

00:52:29.700 --> 00:52:37.180
perhaps a -- an emotional 
rounding of the experience

00:52:37.180 --> 00:52:40.880
that we all had 
in the first three films.

00:52:40.880 --> 00:52:45.060
Hamill: I was just glad I didn't have to go 
to Tosche Station and pick up any power converters.

00:52:45.060 --> 00:52:47.060
[Hardwick laughs]
[Audience cheering]

00:52:50.860 --> 00:52:53.500
Fisher: I've placed information vital 
to the survival of the Rebellion

00:52:53.500 --> 00:52:56.180
in the memory systems 
of this R2 unit.

00:52:56.180 --> 00:52:57.860
I don't want 
to do that again.

00:52:57.860 --> 00:53:00.080
But I didn't have to. 
We sort of, uh --

00:53:00.080 --> 00:53:02.080
We were more 
grown up this time.

00:53:02.080 --> 00:53:04.260
I think --
Ford: It was -- yeah.

00:53:04.260 --> 00:53:07.200
I didn't have to say 
"it'll take a few minutes for the navi-computer --"

00:53:07.200 --> 00:53:09.200
[Fisher joins in]
"-- to calculate the coordinates!"

00:53:09.200 --> 00:53:11.000
[Laughter]

00:53:11.000 --> 00:53:13.100
Hamill: My favorite.
Ford: Thank you, Larry.

00:53:13.100 --> 00:53:14.800
[Hamill laughing]

00:53:14.800 --> 00:53:16.900
Hardwick: Thank you for your question.
Hi, what's your name?

00:53:16.900 --> 00:53:21.320
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Daniel. I had a different question, 
but seeing the cast here, I have to ask.

00:53:21.320 --> 00:53:23.320
Um --

00:53:23.320 --> 00:53:25.320
Thank you for coming.

00:53:25.320 --> 00:53:27.320
Fisher: That's a good question.

00:53:27.320 --> 00:53:29.320
[Hardwick et al laugh]
What I have to -- oh.

00:53:29.320 --> 00:53:33.500
&gt;&gt; Thinking back, 
do you ever think that you --

00:53:33.500 --> 00:53:39.860
what were your expectations of what your characters were going to end up in, before the movie?

00:53:39.860 --> 00:53:42.400
Like, where did you think 
Han Solo would end up?

00:53:42.400 --> 00:53:44.400
Where did you think 
Luke would have ended up?

00:53:44.400 --> 00:53:45.660
And so forth.

00:53:45.660 --> 00:53:48.580
Fisher: 'So forth'? That's all 
I am to you, is 'so forth'?

00:53:48.580 --> 00:53:50.060
[Laughter]

00:53:50.060 --> 00:53:51.740
Hardwick: Oh my God!
Fisher: That's exactly --

00:53:51.740 --> 00:53:54.200
Hardwick: You just Gilligan's-Island her!
"And the rest."

00:53:54.200 --> 00:53:56.700
That's terrible.

00:53:56.700 --> 00:53:59.700
Fisher: I thought it was going 
to be more girly this time,

00:53:59.700 --> 00:54:01.700
and we would do, 
like, a shopping planet.

00:54:01.700 --> 00:54:03.980
[Laughter]
And, um --

00:54:03.980 --> 00:54:08.060
facials. Very weird 
laser hair removal.

00:54:08.060 --> 00:54:11.360
[Laughter]
Ah, but it turned out differently.

00:54:11.360 --> 00:54:12.880
So, that was ok, too.

00:54:14.640 --> 00:54:17.200
Hardwick: Mark?
[Fisher laughs]

00:54:19.260 --> 00:54:24.120
Hamill: Well, I don't know. I mean, 
when you find out that the only...

00:54:24.120 --> 00:54:27.580
woman that you fell head over 
heels with is your sister...

00:54:27.580 --> 00:54:29.380
Hardwick: Yeah.
[Laughter]

00:54:29.380 --> 00:54:33.000
Tough.
Hamill: You know, it's telling you something.

00:54:33.000 --> 00:54:37.720
So, I had a feeling I'd probably 
wind up like Sir Alec in the first one,

00:54:37.720 --> 00:54:40.920
living in some igloo 
out in the desert.

00:54:40.920 --> 00:54:42.920
Fisher: In therapy.
Hamill: In just -- yeah, in therapy,

00:54:42.920 --> 00:54:46.620
but with, uh, just, 
no human contact,

00:54:46.620 --> 00:54:49.600
just droids.
[Laughter]

00:54:49.600 --> 00:54:51.160
Hardwick: You know -- 
first question on a date.

00:54:51.160 --> 00:54:52.860
"You're not my sister, are you?" 
"No." "Okay, good."

00:54:52.860 --> 00:54:55.700
Hamill: Yeah, exactly.
And I want a blood test.

00:54:55.700 --> 00:54:58.000
[Laughter]

00:54:58.000 --> 00:55:00.260
No, seriously, 'cause that's --
I mean, we all laugh about it,

00:55:00.260 --> 00:55:03.380
but can you -- when you follow 
it through, that must have been...

00:55:03.380 --> 00:55:05.380
quite the traumatic experience.

00:55:05.380 --> 00:55:06.980
[Laughter]

00:55:06.980 --> 00:55:09.600
For my character.
You know what I'm saying?

00:55:09.600 --> 00:55:11.540
'Cause we were 
both after her,

00:55:11.540 --> 00:55:13.540
in -- in --
Ford: I know what you're saying.

00:55:13.540 --> 00:55:15.540
[Laughter]

00:55:21.840 --> 00:55:24.160
I mean, how many times 
can I say I'm sorry?

00:55:24.160 --> 00:55:27.060
[Hamill mock-groans]
[Laughter]

00:55:27.060 --> 00:55:29.060
[Hamill mock-groans again]

00:55:29.060 --> 00:55:30.960
Hamill: I'll get over it someday.

00:55:30.960 --> 00:55:32.660
Fisher: My sister, my mother --

00:55:32.660 --> 00:55:34.660
[Hamill joins in]
my sister, my mother.

00:55:34.660 --> 00:55:37.620
[Laughter]

00:55:37.620 --> 00:55:40.940
Hardwick: Harrison, do you have a thought 
about where Han might have ended up?

00:55:40.940 --> 00:55:45.820
Ford: Uh, no, it never occurred to think 
about it, because I never expected, uh,

00:55:45.820 --> 00:55:48.160
that it would, uh,
come back...

00:55:50.960 --> 00:55:53.660
to bless me, 
rather than haunt me.

00:55:53.660 --> 00:55:56.180
It was, uh --
[Laughter]

00:55:56.180 --> 00:55:58.620
I never thought 
that we would do another,

00:55:58.620 --> 00:56:03.100
and it was only, um --
[clears throat]

00:56:03.100 --> 00:56:05.780
You know, the -- it was --
I was very gratified,

00:56:05.780 --> 00:56:08.480
when I read the script,

00:56:08.480 --> 00:56:12.440
because I read something 
that I thought was

00:56:12.440 --> 00:56:14.440
really remarkable,

00:56:14.440 --> 00:56:18.320
really well --
well-written.

00:56:18.320 --> 00:56:20.140
And, uh --

00:56:20.140 --> 00:56:23.140
with some very 
intriguing developments.

00:56:23.140 --> 00:56:26.220
And, so I was delighted 
to be involved.

00:56:26.220 --> 00:56:30.380
And I was -- very happy 
to be a part of this --

00:56:30.380 --> 00:56:34.480
part of the story, 
and the cast again.

00:56:34.480 --> 00:56:37.080
Hardwick: Excellent. 
Well, I want to --

00:56:37.080 --> 00:56:40.380
[Audience cheering]
A huge hand for Star Wars: The Force Awakens.

00:56:40.380 --> 00:56:42.380
[Audience cheering]

00:56:48.780 --> 00:56:49.800
And --

00:56:51.900 --> 00:56:57.820
I feel like this wouldn't be 
the Star Wars panel that you would be expecting,

00:56:57.820 --> 00:57:00.160
if we just ended right there.

00:57:00.160 --> 00:57:02.160
Um -- exactly!

00:57:02.160 --> 00:57:04.160
[Audience cheering]

00:57:04.160 --> 00:57:06.520
Amen to you, sister.
Uh, so --

00:57:06.520 --> 00:57:10.280
[Ridley laughs]
JJ., what else do you have in store?

00:57:10.280 --> 00:57:11.960
Abrams: Well, here's a question.
Um, you know,

00:57:11.960 --> 00:57:15.700
obviously, besides 
these amazing performers,

00:57:15.700 --> 00:57:18.580
on this panel, and again, 
thank you all for being here,

00:57:18.580 --> 00:57:24.460
uh, there's another significant 
star of this series, and it's the music.

00:57:24.460 --> 00:57:25.620
[Woman in audience 
yells "BB-8!"]

00:57:25.620 --> 00:57:27.260
Ridley: Hah, BB-8!

00:57:27.260 --> 00:57:29.660
Abrams: Who likes the music 
to the Star Wars movies?

00:57:29.660 --> 00:57:33.220
[Audience clapping
and cheering]

00:57:33.220 --> 00:57:38.140
So, like, wouldn't it be cool
if we did something insane right now?

00:57:38.140 --> 00:57:39.760
[Audience yells "Yes!"]

00:57:39.760 --> 00:57:42.900
Who wants to go see 
a live Star Wars concert right now?

00:57:42.900 --> 00:57:45.960
[Audience cheering]

00:57:45.960 --> 00:57:47.660
Hardwick: I see 
a few hands are up.

00:57:47.660 --> 00:57:49.360
Abrams: Ok, now --
Hardwick: I see a couple hands are up.

00:57:49.360 --> 00:57:51.800
Abrams: We only have room 
for all of you, so.

00:57:51.800 --> 00:57:53.320
[Audience cheering]

00:57:53.320 --> 00:57:56.620
So here's the thing. We're gonna 
do something that is truly crazy.

00:57:56.620 --> 00:58:00.560
And the city of San Diego 
somehow said okay to this.

00:58:00.560 --> 00:58:04.460
We are literally all going to walk

00:58:04.460 --> 00:58:06.220
to a location together,

00:58:06.220 --> 00:58:09.180
and we are going to experience 
a Star Wars concert, tonight.

00:58:09.180 --> 00:58:11.600
Hardwick: Now --
[Audience cheering]

00:58:11.600 --> 00:58:14.440
[Audience still cheering]
Hardwick: Now --

00:58:14.440 --> 00:58:17.760
Abrams: Now, in order for this to work,

00:58:17.760 --> 00:58:21.140
in order for this to work,
this has to be done very carefully,

00:58:21.140 --> 00:58:23.840
and I put all the responsibility 
on Chris Hardwick's shoulders. Chris!

00:58:23.840 --> 00:58:26.480
Hardwick: Ok, well sure! 
 Thank you!

00:58:26.480 --> 00:58:29.780
I know, cut to tomorrow, like,
'Why am I the one getting sued?'

00:58:29.780 --> 00:58:32.560
Ok, so, here's 
a very important thing.

00:58:32.560 --> 00:58:34.000
Here's what you 
must understand.

00:58:34.000 --> 00:58:36.640
There is room for everyone,

00:58:36.640 --> 00:58:39.360
no murdering allowed --
[Laughter]

00:58:39.360 --> 00:58:41.360
there's no need to push,

00:58:41.360 --> 00:58:45.960
but in a very orderly fashion,
outside, you're gonna get,

00:58:45.960 --> 00:58:49.280
one of these, and this is going to --

00:58:49.280 --> 00:58:51.280
[Audience cheering]

00:58:51.280 --> 00:58:54.160
gain you entrance.

00:58:54.160 --> 00:59:01.700
Um, I first want to quickly introduce 
and give a tremendous amount of respect

00:59:01.700 --> 00:59:05.000
to a man by the name of Kevin Doyle,
I don't know if you say Kevin's story,

00:59:05.000 --> 00:59:11.220
but in memory of his wife, 
Kevin walked 645 miles from Rancho Obi-Wan.

00:59:11.220 --> 00:59:13.040
[Applause]

00:59:13.040 --> 00:59:15.280
And he's now going to 
finish his walk here with us.

00:59:15.280 --> 00:59:17.280
[Audience clapping 
and cheering]

00:59:19.440 --> 00:59:20.460
So,

00:59:22.040 --> 00:59:24.960
Kevin is here, 
and then, uh --

00:59:24.960 --> 00:59:27.660
we all -- here he is, right here, 
here's Kevin. Hello, Kevin.

00:59:27.660 --> 00:59:29.660
Please, a huge --
[Audience cheering]

00:59:37.020 --> 00:59:39.020
Kevin, I promise --

00:59:43.280 --> 00:59:44.820
And some friends.

00:59:44.820 --> 00:59:46.820
Ridley: Oh my gosh. Oh my gosh.

00:59:46.820 --> 00:59:48.620
Hardwick: Kevin, I know 
you thought you were done,

00:59:48.620 --> 00:59:50.620
we're just gonna make 
you walk a little bit more,

00:59:50.620 --> 00:59:52.360
they're gonna follow you,
so here's what --

00:59:52.360 --> 00:59:55.140
in order for this to work, 
everyone has to --

00:59:55.140 --> 00:59:57.220
I know you're taking pictures, 
and you're not listening to me,

00:59:57.220 --> 01:00:00.080
and I feel like you're my children right now, 
and I need you to focus.

01:00:00.080 --> 01:00:02.580
Put down your damn computers, 
and let's -- ok.

01:00:02.580 --> 01:00:04.480
Ok, pictures, 
pictures, pictures.

01:00:04.480 --> 01:00:06.920
Ok, listen to me.
[Woman in audience yell something]

01:00:06.920 --> 01:00:08.280
Ok, good, thank you.

01:00:08.280 --> 01:00:11.220
Um, you're gonna follow these Stormtroopers 
in an orderly fashion,

01:00:11.220 --> 01:00:13.220
there is absolutely 
no need to push,

01:00:13.220 --> 01:00:15.940
uh, because we don't 
want this to turn into a tragic event,

01:00:15.940 --> 01:00:17.780
we want to be happy, 
we're all happy that we're here,

01:00:17.780 --> 01:00:19.660
we're seeing stuff 
no one's ever seen before,

01:00:19.660 --> 01:00:22.640
and we're all gonna go see 
a Star Wars concert together, everyone,

01:00:22.640 --> 01:00:25.220
so please --
[Audience cheering]

01:00:25.220 --> 01:00:27.220
I'm going to let our panel go.

01:00:27.220 --> 01:00:30.120
Would you please leap to your feet 
and give me a huge round of applause

01:00:30.120 --> 01:00:32.060
for the Star Wars: 
The Force Awakens.

01:00:32.060 --> 01:00:34.060
Oh, I'm sorry.
[Audience cheering]

01:00:37.920 --> 01:00:40.740
And honestly, J.J --

